#
# The card is written once; everything after it arrives over Ethernet.
#
# THIS IS A TEMPLATE.  `linux/mksd.sh` writes `uEnv.txt` from it with
# @SERVERIP@ replaced by the TFTP server's address, read from `linux/local.conf`
# --- which is gitignored, because this repository is public and the address
# of a machine on a private network is not part of the project.
#
# U-Boot's preboot loads this from the card's FAT partition and imports it,
# then `uenvboot` imports it a second time and runs `uenvcmd`.  Two imports,
# and the order matters more than once below.
#
# THE COMMAND ITSELF IS NOT HERE.  `uenvcmd` fetches `uEnv.net` from the TFTP
# server,
# imports it, and runs the `netcmd` it defines.  The first card carried the
# whole boot command, and it was wrong twice over on the first boot --- the
# kernel loaded at 256 MB, which made that kernel forget the memory below it,
# and a loose `zImage` that turned out to be a different build from the one
# inside `image.ub` and dies under any tree --- and each correction would have
# meant a card write.  This way the card carries one line that only says where
# to ask, and the answer lives in the served `uEnv.net` beside the tree.
#
# WHY A FAILED FETCH REBOOTS THE BOARD, AND NEVER BOOTS THE STOCK IMAGE
#
# `default_bootcmd` is `run uenvboot; run cp_kernel2ram && bootm ${netstart}`
# --- a semicolon, not an `&&` --- so when uenvcmd fails, U-Boot goes on to
# `cp_kernel2ram`, which is `mmcinfo && fatload mmc 0 ${netstart}
# ${kernel_img}`, and boots the card's `image.ub` with Digilent's own device
# tree and 512 MB: a Linux that owns the CADR's memory.  Mete decided the board
# must never do that --- better stuck than wrong.  This U-Boot has no `while`,
# so the way to wait is to reboot and try again, and the way to make that
# certain is to REPLACE THE FALLBACK'S OWN COPY STEP: this file is imported
# before `default_bootcmd` runs, so the line below is what `run cp_kernel2ram`
# finds.  A fetch that fails therefore ends in `resetting ...`, U-Boot's
# banner ten seconds later, and another try, for as long as the server is
# away; the first boot that completes is the reserved one.
#
# `|| reset` on the end of uenvcmd was tried first and IS NOT ENOUGH: when the
# server's host is up but nothing listens, this U-Boot prints "TFTP server
# died; starting again" and the rest of the `&&`/`||` list does not run at
# all --- measured 10 Sep, the board fell through to image.ub with the reset
# right there on the line.  The variable below caught the same case:
# "starting again", "resetting ...", banner.  With the server answering, the
# override is never reached and the boot is unchanged; measured too.
cp_kernel2ram=reset
#
# WHY THERE IS NO ipaddr HERE
#
# preboot ends in an unconditional `dhcp`, on every boot, whatever this file
# says --- it is the last command of a string that was parsed before we were
# imported.  There is a DHCP server on this LAN, so it answers and sets ipaddr
# from yiaddr; the DHCP server can pin the board's address by its MAC, which
# the console prints.  On a network without one, add ipaddr, netmask, and
# `bootpretryperiod=1000`: that last one does take effect on the same boot,
# because this import happens before the `dhcp` in the same command string
# (net/bootp.c line 716).
#
# WHY serverip SURVIVES THAT dhcp
#
# `CONFIG_BOOTP_SERVERIP` is defined (platform-auto.h line 107), and in
# net/bootp.c the whole serverip-and-bootfile block of `store_net_params()` is
# inside `#if !defined(CONFIG_BOOTP_SERVERIP)`.  A DHCP reply therefore sets
# ipaddr and leaves serverip alone.  The second import re-applies it after the
# dhcp in any case.  The compiled-in default is 172.16.119.130, which is not
# this network.
serverip=@SERVERIP@

# WHY loadbootenv_addr MOVES, AND WHY ONLY THE SECOND IMPORT OBEYS IT
#
# preboot sets it to 0x1EE00000, which is 115 MiB inside the 128 MB the CADR
# owns --- in the spare region past the display.  Because this file is imported
# before `uenvboot` runs its own `load`, the value below is what the second
# load uses.  The first load already happened at 0x1EE00000 and nothing on the
# card can change that.  0x0f800000 is clear of the reservation and of the
# tree at 0x0f000000; `uEnv.net` is fetched to the same address afterwards,
# this file having been imported by then.
loadbootenv_addr=0x0f800000

# 1468 rather than the 512-byte default (RFC 2348).  This build has
# tftpblocksize and tftptimeout; it has no tftpwindowsize, which postdates it.
tftpblocksize=1468

# `env import -t` reads a text file of `name=value` lines, which is what
# `uEnv.net` is; `${filesize}` is what the tftpboot before it just set.  If
# anything in the chain fails, U-Boot falls through to `cp_kernel2ram`, which
# is the reset above.  Everything after this line is decided on the server.
uenvcmd=tftpboot 0x0f800000 uEnv.net && env import -t 0x0f800000 ${filesize} && run netcmd
